  "answer": "Read only, no rule. Both reads agree in all 7 setups. A second phone edit changed the DOM order in 4 of 7: f1 (e8 again), f2 (the resize makes no chain), f5 (the edits the other way round) and f7 (two blocks). Each time it was a2, resized on the phone at that edit or the one before, and overlapping a1, and a2 went ahead of a1 (z2, z1 and then z3). It made no change when no block had been resized: f3 (a2 moved down 1, still on a1), f4 (a3 moved up onto a2, a chain of three) and f6 (the first edit apart). So a chain is not needed, and a move can reorder once the block has been resized. Ours sorts by phone row after a section's first phone edit, so it misses f1, f2, f5 and f7. A simple rule, that a resized block goes ahead of a lower-z block it overlaps, fits these 7. It misses seen reads in CAP-A2CORPUS1STEPS (s5 also brings z3 first, and s6 moves z3 last) and in CAP-A2CORPUS2 (p1, p3 and p4), so no rule is fitted and no predictions round was run."
